Z||000000||Philphine||09-02-2001||11:01 PM||||<BR>i haven't done much with distributers, and what i have done is more with old point gap ones. i want to replace the cap, rotor and some or all of the other works but except for the dist. cap, none of the rest will come off (didn't try the module). shouldn't the rotor just pull off? i looked through my hanes for some info but it dosen't say anything about a clip or something holding it. <P>all the works look pretty rusty and somewhat corroded. is the rotor just stuck? i tried to pry it a little but didn't want to break it if there's something else holding it. i'm trying to avoid having to take the whole thing out so i won't have to worry about getting it back in time again. thanks for any help. <P>------------------<BR>Phil T.<P>you can't win. you can't break even and you can't get out of the game.||||reg||1
Z||000001||2birds||09-02-2001||11:13 PM||||The rotor just slips over the end of the shaft, and it is indexed by a slot 180 degrees out from the point of the rotor.  I sometimes have to pry mine off.<P>Ever replaced the pickup coil?  It's a good idea if it is getting along in age.  You have to remove the driveshaft via the top to do this.  Leave the reluctor wheel alone.  There is a simple roll pin that holds the distributor drive gear onto the drive shaft, and you just push it out with a skinny punch (re-use the pin after reassembly).  Once the gear is off, all you do is work the driveshaft out from the top.  Old oil gets caked onto the shaft, so I cleaned mine up with 400 grit wet/dry sandpaper and oiled it up, and it went right back in easily. <P>Ignition module is the only other part, and if it has spark, it works.  I wouldn't mess with it, otherwise.||||reg||1
Z||000002||beer_stud_21||09-02-2001||11:16 PM||||Phil,<P>The rotor just pulls off.  Yours must just me <B>really</B> on there good.  the module come off with those two small screws.  Other than that you have to pull the distributor from the block to do anything else.  I'm definitely a beginner mechanic and I pulled mine and replaced it just fine.  Just use a pencil or lumber crayon to mark where the rotor is pointing and also mark the body of the distributor at the block so that you can put it back in just how it came out.  The pickup coil that you see inside there with the two wires comin should definitely be replaced if you do pull the dist.  They are less than $10.00 at Napa.  If you have a multimeter you can test it to see if its bad without pulling the dist.  I can get you the instructions from my Haynes manual (or is it in Chiltons?  i cant remember.)  if you  need.<P>-Marlin<P>OOPS... Looks like 2birds beat me to the punch....  Can't blame me for tryin can you?  hehe<p>[This message has been edited by beer_stud_21 (edited 09-02-2001).]||||reg||1
Z||000003||Philphine||09-02-2001||11:30 PM||||ok. thanks for the info. guess i'll pry at it some more tomorrow. this is the '86 gt i just got and it seems like the distributer is giving me the most problem so far. thanks again.||||reg||1
